.TE
.SS Working With Files
.SS Opening File Sets
.PP
Press \f[V]B\f[R] to open a new file or file set.
The prompt accepts one or more file names, shell globs such as
\f[V]*.go\f[R], quoted filenames containing spaces, and history entries.
It also expands special symbols such as \f[V]%\f[R] for the current file
and \f[V]\[ti]\f[R] for the home directory.
.PP
When a file set is opened with \f[V]B\f[R], \f[B]browse\f[R] temporarily
suspends the current list.
After the nested list is finished, \f[B]browse\f[R] resumes the previous
list at the prior location.
.SS Showing the Current List
.PP
Press \f[V]a\f[R] to display the current file and any remaining files in
the active list.
For example, if the session began with:
.nf

browse file1 file2 file3
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
and \f[V]file2\f[R] is currently displayed, pressing \f[V]a\f[R] shows
\f[V]file2\f[R] and \f[V]file3\f[R].
.SS Showing the Browse Stack
.PP
Press \f[V]A\f[R] to display the current file together with suspended
parent files.
The most immediately resumable parent is listed first; for example,
while viewing \f[V]file3\f[R] after opening \f[V]file2\f[R] from
\f[V]file1\f[R], browse shows \f[V][file3] file2 file1\f[R].
.SS Re-Reading Files
.PP
Press \f[V]R\f[R] to re-read the current file from disk.
This is useful when a file is rewritten in place, replaced, truncated,
or otherwise modified in a way not fully captured by automatic file
tracking.
.PP
For example:
.nf

mv log log.old
app > log
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
When the current file is moved away or removed, \f[B]browse\f[R]
continues reading from the already open file descriptor and reports the
rescue path in use.
If a new file appears at the original path, press \f[V]R\f[R] to return
to that path and rebuild the browse state from disk.
If the original path is replaced with a different file, \f[B]browse\f[R]
detects the inode change and automatically reopens the path.
If the file is truncated, \f[B]browse\f[R] clears the old offsets,
prints \f[V]File truncated\f[R], and begins reading the shortened file
from the beginning.
.SS Recovering a Deleted File
.PP
Removing a file does not immediately destroy its contents while a
process still holds it open.
When the file being browsed is removed, \f[B]browse\f[R] duplicates its
open descriptor and continues reading, so the file remains readable and
can still be followed.
.PP
\f[B]browse\f[R] reports the descriptor from which the file was rescued:
.nf

File removed: recover from /proc/6620/fd/7
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
That path belongs to the \f[B]browse\f[R] process itself rather than to
the invoking shell, so it remains valid for shell escapes started with
\f[V]!\f[R].
The displayed filename also becomes the rescue path, which means
\f[V]%\f[R] expands to it and the contents can be written back to disk:
.nf

!cp % \[ti]/recovered.log
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Recovery must occur while the file is still open.
\f[B]browse\f[R] closes the rescued descriptor when you move to another
file or leave the session, and the contents are no longer recoverable
after that.
.PP
If a new file later appears at the original path, \f[B]browse\f[R]
reports \f[V]File removed: press R to re-read\f[R], leaving the timing
of the switch to the user.
.SS Rewinding Lists
.PP
Press \f[V]Ctrl+R\f[R] to rewind the active browse list.
This returns to the first file in the current list, including a nested
list opened with \f[V]B\f[R], without rewinding any parent list.
.SS Browsing Compressed Files
.PP
\f[B]browse\f[R] detects compressed files by their magic bytes and
decompresses them transparently.
A compressed file may be opened in the same manner as any other file,
either from the command line or with \f[V]B\f[R] inside an existing
session.
.PP
Supported formats:
.PP
